================================================================================================
What changes once the policy is applied
================================================================================================

  78 tests in 15 families; 45 significant uncorrected, 33 after correction.

  Loses significance under its family's correction:
    - real vs phase-perm (0.8% changed) (label controls): p = 0.0480 -> 0.1920
    - HRV (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0174 -> 0.2610
    - theta/beta (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0280 -> 0.3920
    - calibration level (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0293 -> 0.3920
    - beta (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0456 -> 0.4620
    - calibration volatility (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0381 -> 0.4572
    - calibration IQR (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0420 -> 0.4620
    - HRV SD (calibration screen (17 measures)): p = 0.0485 -> 0.4620
    - rate of change of arousal (within-trial control): p = 0.0261 -> 0.0783
    - random slope on arousal (LRT) (reliability): p = 0.0384 -> 0.0768
    - above-band x full BCI on 5 s hazard (feedback effects): p = 0.0400 -> 0.0800
    - hard: BCI vs half-sham (trajectory clusters): p = 0.0228 -> 0.0912

  Notes on the policy:
   - HRV is reported as an a-priori test and is NOT corrected against
     the 17-measure screen it was not part of. Respiration comes FROM
     that screen and is reported with its corrected p-value. HRV also
     appears inside the screen, where it does not survive; both are
     shown rather than the more convenient one.
   - Table 1's four metrics are monotone transforms of one another,
     so correcting them as four independent tests is conservative to
     the point of being misleading; report two.
   - Cluster p-values already control error across time bins; the
     correction applied here is across the eight trajectory tests.

